I hate to say it, but once again i'm let down by developers ♥♥♥♥ing over their own masterpieces. So i’m going to rate this game on two levels;
The gameplay: 10/10
This game offers one of the most unique and incredible experience through its one-of-a-kind gameplay, and its fast paced style. Between my two accounts, first with hi-rez client, and then on steam, i've clocked several hundred hours and haven't grown bored with the gameplay. No matter what level you are there's always room to improve, always that millisecond to react sooner, there's no mastering this, and I think that's what makes it so worthwhile. You start off completely clueless, barely managing the movement and the combat physics, and before you know it you're doing things you didn't think possible. Tribes: Ascend was one of the most rewarding games i've played in the aspect of improving yourself, and I don't think any other game comes close. But alas, even with near perfect games, there's always a dark side, which brings me to my second point;
Development: 0/10
You'd think when given a gameplay gem such as Tribes, a developer would be able to take consumer feedback and make a commercial diamond for the industry to strive after, but just the opposite has happened. Through awful customer support, ♥♥♥♥♥♥ anti-cheat, and dreaded microtransactions up the wazoo, they managed to tarnish a legacy that could have been remembered for decades. Abandoning this game for SMITE, Hi-Rez managed to catalogue themselves in the likes of publishers like EA, and late Valve. Blindly following the money, it seems they no longer care about what makes games great. Now Paladins is here, in a futile effort to compete against big names like Blizzard, something they should have learned was impossible from the utter fail of Battleborn. (and yes I wasted my time in that game too, also disappointed in a what was actually a really good game being ruined by ♥♥♥♥♥♥ devs) I guess by the end of this i’m supposed to have reviewed Tribes: Ascend, but it’s not as simple as that. I love this game. I also hate it. I want to recommend it, but I feel like avoiding it entirely will save you from this amalgam of emotions that I and many others who played it were forced to go through. So I would say save yourself the trouble, and forget this amazing genre of gaming. Forget this one of a kind masterpiece that nothing else can compare to, and just settle with the average shooters and mobas where you won't be so disappointed.
-Conniverse

